WEBVTT

00:00.150 --> 00:02.280
Instructor: So, the next piece of the .git folder

00:02.280 --> 00:05.220
we'll cover is the refs folder.

00:05.220 --> 00:07.440
So, in every git repo that you make,

00:07.440 --> 00:09.480
there's going to be a refs folder

00:09.480 --> 00:11.850
and inside of that refs folder,

00:11.850 --> 00:14.190
there are a couple of different sub directories.

00:14.190 --> 00:17.760
And, we'll start by talking about the heads directory.

00:17.760 --> 00:21.240
But, actually, before that, refs for references

00:21.240 --> 00:23.160
is where git stores all of the-

00:23.160 --> 00:25.500
every time I've talked about a pointer or a reference,

00:25.500 --> 00:28.890
whether it's a branch pointer or a tag,

00:28.890 --> 00:31.020
two different ways, or two things, in git

00:31.020 --> 00:34.020
that refer to commits, those are stored,

00:34.020 --> 00:37.320
they actually exist, in the refs folder.

00:37.320 --> 00:39.930
So, that's what refs is: references.

00:39.930 --> 00:42.240
So, we'll start by taking a look at branches

00:42.240 --> 00:45.540
in this refs/heads directory.

00:45.540 --> 00:49.440
Git makes one file for every branch in a repo

00:49.440 --> 00:51.540
and that file will have the name of the branch.

00:51.540 --> 00:54.000
So, when I've shown diagrams like this in the past,

00:54.000 --> 00:56.460
where I've shown, or I've illustrated,

00:56.460 --> 00:58.500
branch references with an arrow,

00:58.500 --> 01:00.300
like, a label and an arrow,

01:00.300 --> 01:03.420
these references are just files in that folder,

01:03.420 --> 01:06.300
in the refs/heads folder.

01:06.300 --> 01:08.940
So, if we had a branch called dark mode,

01:08.940 --> 01:11.370
there is a file called dark mode,

01:11.370 --> 01:12.720
and there's no arrow,

01:12.720 --> 01:16.170
but inside the file there's a commit hash.

01:16.170 --> 01:18.210
It points to a particular commit.

01:18.210 --> 01:20.850
So, in this repo, this is the react repo,

01:20.850 --> 01:24.300
let's see, we've got three branches here

01:24.300 --> 01:28.500
and if I just show you inside of this .git folder,

01:28.500 --> 01:31.950
I'll just show the contents, .git/,

01:31.950 --> 01:34.350
and then we've got refs right here.

01:34.350 --> 01:37.380
So, I'll just print out the contents.

01:37.380 --> 01:40.410
We have heads, remotes, and tags.

01:40.410 --> 01:42.270
Now, you may not have all of these

01:42.270 --> 01:43.740
depending on if you have any remotes,

01:43.740 --> 01:45.360
if you have any tags.

01:45.360 --> 01:46.920
Also, I forgot to mention that,

01:46.920 --> 01:49.650
if you started with a brand new empty git repo,

01:49.650 --> 01:51.210
things are slightly different

01:51.210 --> 01:52.980
than if you actually have a commit in there,

01:52.980 --> 01:55.620
if you have, you know, stuff being staged,

01:55.620 --> 01:56.790
if you have history.

01:56.790 --> 01:59.010
So, everything I'm talking about is

01:59.010 --> 02:03.240
once you have at least one commit and at least one branch.

02:03.240 --> 02:06.990
Okay, so inside of this heads directory,

02:06.990 --> 02:09.360
I'll print that out just like this,

02:09.360 --> 02:13.740
you'll see we have three files, one for each branch.

02:13.740 --> 02:18.150
And, each one of those files contains a commit hash.

02:18.150 --> 02:20.460
